            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is NuGet?</h2>
                    <p>NuGet is the package manager for .NET. It's how you add third-party libraries to your projects—everything from JSON serializers to database drivers to logging frameworks.</p>
                    
                    <p>The official repository at <a href="https://nuget.org" target="_blank">nuget.org</a> hosts over 350,000 packages, making it easy to find solutions for almost any problem.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Finding Packages</h2>
                    <p>There are several ways to discover NuGet packages:</p>
                    
                    <h3>NuGet.org Website</h3>
                    <p>Browse <a href="https://nuget.org" target="_blank">nuget.org</a> to search for packages, view documentation, and check download statistics.</p>

                    <h3>Command Line</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Search for packages
dotnet package search json

# Search with detailed output
dotnet package search serilog --take 10</code></pre>

                    <h3>Popular Packages</h3>
                    <div class="package-grid">
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>Newtonsoft.Json</h4>
                            <p>JSON serialization (legacy, but widely used)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>Serilog</h4>
                            <p>Structured logging</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>Dapper</h4>
                            <p>Lightweight ORM</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>AutoMapper</h4>
                            <p>Object-to-object mapping</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>FluentValidation</h4>
                            <p>Validation library</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="package-card">
                            <h4>Polly</h4>
                            <p>Resilience and transient fault handling</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Adding Packages</h2>
                    <p>Use the <code>dotnet add package</code> command:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Add the latest stable version
dotnet add package Newtonsoft.Json

# Add a specific version
dotnet add package Serilog --version 3.1.1

# Add a prerelease version
dotnet add package SomePackage --prerelease

# Add to a specific project
dotnet add src/MyApp/MyApp.csproj package Newtonsoft.Json</code></pre>

                    <p>This adds the package to your <code>.csproj</code> file:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Package Versions</h2>
                    <p>Understanding version numbers helps you manage updates safely:</p>

                    <div class="version-example">
                        <div class="version-number">
                            <span class="major">13</span>.<span class="minor">0</span>.<span class="patch">3</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="version-labels">
                            <span>Major</span>
                            <span>Minor</span>
                            <span>Patch</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Major:</strong> Breaking changes—may require code modifications</li>
                        <li><strong>Minor:</strong> New features, backward compatible</li>
                        <li><strong>Patch:</strong> Bug fixes, backward compatible</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Version Ranges</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Exact version --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="Pkg" Version="1.2.3" /&gt;

&lt;!-- Minimum version --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="Pkg" Version="1.2.3" /&gt;

&lt;!-- Floating version (latest patch) --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="Pkg" Version="1.2.*" /&gt;

&lt;!-- Range --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="Pkg" Version="[1.0,2.0)" /&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Managing Packages</h2>

                    <h3>List Installed Packages</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># List all packages in a project
dotnet list package

# List packages for entire solution
dotnet list MySolution.sln package

# Check for outdated packages
dotnet list package --outdated

# Include transitive dependencies
dotnet list package --include-transitive</code></pre>

                    <h3>Update Packages</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Update a specific package
dotnet add package Newtonsoft.Json

# The above command updates to latest if already installed
# There's no explicit "update" command - just add again</code></pre>

                    <h3>Remove Packages</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Remove a package
dotnet remove package Newtonsoft.Json</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Restoring Packages</h2>
                    <p>When you clone a project or your packages get corrupted, restore them:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Restore packages for current project
dotnet restore

# Restore for entire solution
dotnet restore MySolution.sln

# Force restore (ignore cache)
dotnet restore --force</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Note</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><code>dotnet build</code> automatically restores packages, so you rarely need to run <code>dotnet restore</code> manually.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Package Sources</h2>
                    <p>By default, NuGet uses nuget.org. You can add additional sources for private packages:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># List configured sources
dotnet nuget list source

# Add a private source
dotnet nuget add source https://pkgs.mycompany.com/nuget -n MyCompany

# Add with credentials
dotnet nuget add source https://pkgs.mycompany.com/nuget -n MyCompany -u username -p password

# Remove a source
dotnet nuget remove source MyCompany</code></pre>

                    <h3>NuGet.Config</h3>
                    <p>Configure sources in a <code>NuGet.Config</code> file for your project:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?&gt;
&lt;configuration&gt;
  &lt;packageSources&gt;
    &lt;add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" /&gt;
    &lt;add key="MyCompany" value="https://pkgs.mycompany.com/nuget" /&gt;
  &lt;/packageSources&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Central Package Management</h2>
                    <p>For solutions with many projects, centralize version management with <code>Directory.Packages.props</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Directory.Packages.props in solution root --&gt;
&lt;Project&gt;
  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;ManagePackageVersionsCentrally&gt;true&lt;/ManagePackageVersionsCentrally&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
  
  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;PackageVersion Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" /&gt;
    &lt;PackageVersion Include="Serilog" Version="3.1.1" /&gt;
    &lt;PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.6.2" /&gt;
  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <p>Then in your project files, omit the version:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Individual .csproj files --&gt;
&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" /&gt;
  &lt;PackageReference Include="Serilog" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Security Considerations</h2>
                    
                    <div class="warning-box">
                        <div class="warning-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Security Best Practices</span>
                        </div>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Keep packages updated to get security patches</li>
                            <li>Use <code>dotnet list package --vulnerable</code> to check for known vulnerabilities</li>
                            <li>Verify package authors and download counts before installing</li>
                            <li>Consider using a private feed to control which packages are available</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Check for vulnerable packages
dotnet list package --vulnerable

# Check for deprecated packages
dotnet list package --deprecated</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>NuGet is the package manager for .NET with 350,000+ packages on nuget.org.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet add package</code> to install and <code>dotnet remove package</code> to uninstall.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet list package --outdated</code> to find packages that need updating.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Central Package Management for large solutions with many projects.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>

